rue] [17592186433671 1077 "selection" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433671 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433663 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433671 1079 #uuid "b65fa8f3-11c1-5f2d-b151-e84007d15bf9" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433667 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433672 1069 "q-14143" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433672 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-18{x}^{2}+26x-12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433672 1072 #uuid "2e2ad5ba-576c-5412-b8cf-f6ef173b3a57" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433672 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433671 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433673 1069 "q-112864" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433673 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2?$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433673 1072 #uuid "879d12d0-2113-5e81-a5d2-2d55d4183634" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433673 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $2,$ and the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }2}{\\text{factor of}\\,-4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(1)$ is a factor of $2$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $-4.$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433669 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433674 1069 "q-83824" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433674 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-7x+8?$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433674 1072 #uuid "b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433674 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-5$ does not take the above form. The value $-5$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{5}{1},$ and the numerator $(5)$ is not a factor of $8.$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433670 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433675 1069 "q-112977" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433675 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18$ has a root in the interval $(2,4),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433675 1072 #uuid "b9c7c178-4ebe-5318-8cb7-742204e595a0" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433675 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(2,4)$ is $3.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x+2)(x-3) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+3=1.\n$$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433668 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433676 1069 "q-83736" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433676 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+7x-8?$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433676 1072 #uuid "c76ce26c-3285-586a-b0c4-847ce7576f60" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433676 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8,$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-4=-\\frac{4}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(4)$ is a factor of $-8$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $5.$" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433665 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433677 1069 "q-323225" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433677 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+8$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433677 1072 #uuid "e7505886-69c0-558a-a441-b828788cc51e" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true] [17592186433677 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 8,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)" 13194139534713 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-087ad481-79d2-5803-a05b-415265f74035" 17592186433634 "answer-1436fc42-169a-5d6e-8e67-e55cf95c9b4e" 17592186433635 "answer-14dbd20f-dae6-536f-87a7-89593f714dee" 17592186433636 "answer-163d085f-a04f-5649-b71b-e90a4a64a76e" 17592186433637 "answer-19199503-6a7e-5f9f-a197-b117e0b7750b" 17592186433638 "answer-285ade38-532c-552a-8139-54551e46a1fd" 17592186433639 "answer-316eb2af-0429-59de-b344-1be387d9a577" 17592186433640 "answer-3b174001-a899-57c4-9bb5-2ae14ddc8f25" 17592186433641 "answer-54d7c5c3-a9e2-524a-a7d5-fb4012f15478" 17592186433642 "answer-581646f9-25ed-5c5a-aeb0-c470516651f6" 17592186433643 "answer-67e7348d-395b-5ab0-8891-b4eb9d1a0356" 17592186433644 "answer-68ce9788-0470-5c3e-a1b2-2e1c81f8f0d9" 17592186433645 "answer-7b81c397-2f18-5ef5-b521-31182e577fe2" 17592186433646 "answer-7c44e16a-680d-5060-8d29-321f46d27bfa" 17592186433647 "answer-861251cb-82b1-5685-8d4f-6715f30f07d4" 17592186433648 "answer-8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" 17592186433649 "answer-97c4e86d-95a3-53e3-b80a-a14798afdf4e" 17592186433650 "answer-9cb2fbb0-cb33-517d-ab50-a44b4aa0e364" 17592186433651 "answer-b9344a36-f4c2-5ee5-8b24-5bc73e0761f9" 17592186433652 "answer-bd738110-2730-510b-a6fe-9f8c39d2690d" 17592186433653 "answer-c244dcdf-d702-5e3f-8cc2-53ceb79a1e2b" 17592186433654 "answer-cd3b1417-956a-5906-8f67-b13dacbb7ab9" 17592186433655 "answer-cd8039a9-93f2-5720-ab0a-1873075b3cd6" 175921